def starting_page():
    """What a page navigation gets when the desktop has not answered it within
    STARTING_PAGE_AFTER: the session page's own "starting" words, and a wait
    for the desktop's page that ends in a reload of this same address.

    NOTHING ON IT CAN BE PRESSED, and that is the property that keeps this out
    of the arrival rule. It reloads the address it was served at, which is the
    request that was already being made; it mints nothing, resumes nothing and
    chooses nothing. Whatever the session page would have decided (the fresh
    marker, the Connect card) it still decides, when it arrives.

    THE WAIT IS A fetch() OF THE SAME ADDRESS, and the router tells it from the
    navigation by is_page_navigation(): a fetch is held until the desktop
    answers, exactly as the navigation used to be, so there is no second clock
    here to disagree with the ones behind it (hdw4s-wait's, the relay unit's).
    ANY answer ends it -- the desktop's page, or the refusal page when the start
    has failed -- and the reload then shows that answer. A request that dies
    without one (the router's upstream timeout) is simply asked again.

    Without JavaScript the <noscript> refresh does the same job, cruder.

    NOTHING HERE TOUCHES THE STREAM. No client is loaded and no WebSocket is
    opened, so no screen size reaches the desktop from this page: the rule that
    nothing may resize before the end of GNOME's startup is untouched."""
    return card(
        "Your desktop is starting",
        "<div id=\"hdw4s-starting-page\">"
        "<p>It is coming up now. This usually takes a few seconds.</p>"
        "<div class=\"alive\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>"
        "</div>",
        "<script>(function(){function poll(){fetch(location.href,"
        "{cache:'no-store',credentials:'same-origin'}).then(function(){"
        "location.reload();},function(){setTimeout(poll,1000);});}poll();})();"
        "</script>",
        "<noscript><meta http-equiv=\"refresh\" content=\"5\"></noscript>")


def is_page_navigation(method, rest, headers):
    """Is this a browser loading the session PAGE as a document?

    Only such a request may be answered with starting_page(). Everything else
    to a session -- the client's assets, its WebSocket, and above all our own
    page's fetch() of this address -- must reach the desktop, or the wait on
    that page would be answered with the page itself, for ever.

    Sec-Fetch-Dest is what Chrome, Firefox and Safari send today and it is
    decisive when present. Without it, a navigation is the one request that
    asks for text/html; fetch() asks for */*. Not browser-specific either way."""
    if method != b"GET" or rest != b"/":
        return False
    if header_value(headers, "Upgrade") is not None:
        return False
    dest = header_value(headers, "Sec-Fetch-Dest")
    if dest is not None:
        return dest.lower() == b"document"
    return b"text/html" in (header_value(headers, "Accept") or b"").lower()


